\begin{abstract}
We study \emph{framed puzzles}: mechanical interlock systems on $n$ variables in which a variable is realised by a matched pair of pieces, a clause piece exposes finitely many input notches each milled for a specific variable and polarity, and an \emph{assembly} is a choice of one variable piece per variable that lets every clause piece snap into place. Global tab--blank complementation re-mills every notch for the opposite polarity.

We upgrade the classical observation that complementation preserves solvability into an exact statement about complete solution spaces: complementation transports the assembly space of a puzzle onto that of its complement by Boolean negation, so the two are equinumerous via an explicit bijection. Because a Boolean vector is never its own negation, the induced involution on the Boolean cube is fixed-point free whenever $n \ge 1$; consequently the \emph{untagged} union of the two assembly spaces has even cardinality. This settles the conjectured parity law in a strictly stronger form: no non-self-duality hypothesis is required, self-dual puzzles have an even number of assemblies rather than being fixed configurations, and the unique fixed configuration in the theory is the empty assembly on zero variables --- precisely where parity fails.

We replace parity by an explicit orbit decomposition: fixing the polarity of the first variable gives a computable section of the orbit map, so a complement-stable set of assemblies has cardinality exactly twice its gauge. As a by-product, complementation viewed as a permutation of the $2^{n}$-element cube has sign $(-1)^{2^{n-1}}$, hence is odd exactly when $n=1$.

Finally we prove that the parity constraint is the \emph{only} constraint. A single exclusion piece forbids exactly one assembly, so every subset of the Boolean cube is exactly an assembly space, realisable with $2^{n}-|S|$ clause pieces. Consequently the single-puzzle assembly spectrum is all of $\{0,\dots,2^{n}\}$ (odd counts occur); the combined spectrum for $n \ge 1$ is exactly the even numbers $\le 2^{n}$; self-dual assembly spaces occur in every even size $\le 2^{n}$; and there are exactly $2^{2^{n-1}}$ complement-stable assembly spaces, the square root of the total $2^{2^{n}}$. With $d$ interlock depths, the combined assembly space of the $d$ depth-shifts has cardinality exactly $d$ times its gauge, so $d$ divides it; tab--blank parity is the $d=2$ slice.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}

Interlock systems --- jigsaw puzzles, dovetail joints, snap-fit assemblies --- encode their combinatorics in a binary alphabet of complementary shapes. A tab mates with a blank; a tab does not mate with a tab. The mating relation is anti-symmetric in shape: what matters is not which shape is present but that the two shapes at a joint are opposite.

The immediate consequence is that reversing the polarity of \emph{every} interlock simultaneously leaves every joint mating, so solvability is invariant under global complementation. This observation is usually presented as a curiosity.

The present work asks what the observation is a shadow \emph{of}, and answers the question completely. Invariance of solvability is the $\pi_{0}$-level statement of a much finer fact: complementation is an order-two \emph{transport of complete solution spaces}, and because that transport is \emph{free}, it imposes an arithmetic constraint on solution counts. The constraint is exactly one bit --- parity --- and that bit is all it imposes.

\subsection{The conjecture and its correction}

The problem was posed in a hedged form.

\begin{conjecture}[As posed]
For every framed puzzle not isomorphic to its global tab--blank complement, complementation acts freely on the disjoint union of the two assembly spaces, forcing their combined cardinality to be even.
\end{conjecture}

The hedge anticipates that a \emph{self-dual} puzzle might be a fixed point of the symmetry and escape the parity law. We prove the conjecture (Theorem~\ref{thm:conj}) and show that its hypothesis is misdirected. Freeness is a property of the \emph{configuration space}: a Boolean vector is never its own complement, so the involution has no fixed points as soon as $n \ge 1$, whatever the puzzle. Self-duality does not create a fixed point; it collapses the two assembly spaces onto one, on which the involution still acts freely, so the conclusion is \emph{stronger}: such a puzzle has an even number of assemblies (Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdual}). The hypothesis does exactly one thing: it excludes $n=0$, where every puzzle is vacuously self-complementary and where parity genuinely fails.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item Exact transport (Theorem~\ref{thm:transport}): $\A(P^{*}) = \sigma(\A(P))$, hence $|\A(P^{*})| = |\A(P)|$.
\item Untagged parity (Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}): $|\A(P)\cup \A(P^{*})|$ is even for $n \ge 1$, with no non-self-duality hypothesis.
\item Refutation of the self-duality boundary (Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdual}, Section~\ref{sec:sharp}).
\item Orbit decomposition with a computable section (Theorem~\ref{thm:orbit}).
\item Sign of complementation (Theorem~\ref{thm:sign}).
\item Complete expressiveness (Theorem~\ref{thm:express}).
\item Exact spectra (Theorems~\ref{thm:single}, \ref{thm:spectrum}, \ref{thm:selfdualspec}).
\item Density of self-duality (Theorem~\ref{thm:density}).
\item Cyclic generalisation (Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclic}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The framed-puzzle model}

\subsection{Edges, polarity, and mating}

We work over a two-element alphabet of interlock shapes with a fixed-point-free complementation $e \mapsto e^{\mathrm{c}}$, and say two edges \emph{mate} when each is the complement of the other. A truth value is encoded as an edge shape by an injective encoding $\mathrm{enc}$.

\begin{lemma}[Local dictionary]\label{lem:dict}
Let $a$ be a choice of variable pieces and $(i,p)$ a notch milled for variable $i$ and polarity $p$. The notch interlocks with the installed variable-$i$ piece if and only if $a_{i} = p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The installed piece exposes $\mathrm{enc}(a_{i})$ and the notch presents $\mathrm{enc}(p)^{\mathrm{c}}$. Mating means $\mathrm{enc}(a_{i}) = \mathrm{enc}(p)$, equivalent by injectivity to $a_{i} = p$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Puzzles and assemblies}

Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{literal input} (notch) is a pair $\ell = (i,p) \in \{1,\dots,n\}\times\{\T,\F\}$. A \emph{clause piece} is a finite list of literal inputs. A \emph{framed puzzle on $n$ variables} is a finite list $P$ of clause pieces. The frame and the $n$ matched pairs of variable pieces are implicit in the variable set.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
An \emph{assembly} is a point $a \in \cube$. The input $(i,p)$ \emph{fits} under $a$ if $a_{i}=p$. A clause piece \emph{snaps into place} under $a$ if some of its inputs fits. The assembly $a$ \emph{assembles} $P$, written $a \models P$, if every clause piece of $P$ snaps into place. The \emph{assembly space} is
\[
  \A(P) = \{\, a \in \cube \;:\; a \models P \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

Assembly is decidable by finite enumeration and $|\A(P)| \le 2^{n}$. The map $A$ is not injective: puzzles differing by repetition or reordering have the same assembly space, and all statements about self-duality below are at the level of assembly spaces.

\begin{remark}
A notch is a literal, a clause piece a disjunction, a puzzle a CNF formula, an assembly a satisfying assignment. The framed model is a faithful mechanical presentation of Boolean constraint satisfaction in which the symmetry studied here is a physical operation (re-milling), not a syntactic artefact.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Complementation}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{complement} of a clause piece $c$ is $c^{*} = \{(i,\lnot p) : (i,p)\in c\}$, and $P^{*} = \{c^{*} : c \in P\}$. Complementation of assemblies is $\sigma(a)_{i} = \lnot a_{i}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$c \mapsto c^{*}$, $P \mapsto P^{*}$ and $\sigma$ are involutions; in particular $\sigma$ is a bijection.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Freeness on the cube]\label{lem:free}
If $n \ge 1$ then $\sigma(a) \ne a$ for every $a \in \cube$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Evaluate at coordinate $1$: $\sigma(a)_{1} = \lnot a_{1} \ne a_{1}$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:free} is the entire engine of the parity results; it depends only on $n \ge 1$ and not at all on $P$.

\section{A parity principle for free involutions}

\begin{theorem}[Free-involution parity]\label{thm:freeparity}
Let $S$ be a finite set and $g : S \to S$ satisfy $g(g(a)) = a$ and $g(a) \ne a$ for all $a \in S$. Then $|S|$ is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{(i) Signed product.} Consider $\prod_{a \in S}(-1)$ in $\mathbb{Z}$. Pairing $a$ with $g(a)$ is a fixed-point-free involution of the index set under which the factors multiply to $(-1)(-1) = 1$, so the product telescopes to $1$. But it also equals $(-1)^{|S|}$; if $|S|$ were odd this gives $-1 = 1$, a contradiction.

\emph{(ii) Orbit partition.} The orbits of $\langle g \rangle$ have size $1$ or $2$; size $1$ is excluded by freeness; the orbits partition $S$; hence $|S| = 2\cdot(\#\text{orbits})$.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact transport of assembly spaces}

\begin{lemma}[Literal level]\label{lem:lit}
For every assembly $a$ and input $(i,p)$, the complemented notch $(i,\lnot p)$ fits under $\sigma(a)$ if and only if $(i,p)$ fits under $a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma(a)_{i} = \lnot p \iff \lnot a_{i} = \lnot p \iff a_{i} = p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Clause level]\label{lem:clause}
For every assembly $a$ and clause piece $c$, the piece $c^{*}$ snaps into place under $\sigma(a)$ if and only if $c$ snaps into place under $a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Complementation is a bijection from the inputs of $c$ onto those of $c^{*}$; combine with Lemma~\ref{lem:lit} and quantify existentially.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact transport]\label{thm:transport}
For every framed puzzle $P$ on $n$ variables and every assembly $a$,
\[
  \sigma(a) \models P^{*} \iff a \models P .
\]
Consequently $\A(P^{*}) = \sigma(\A(P))$ and $|\A(P^{*})| = |\A(P)|$, and $\sigma$ restricts to an explicit bijection $\A(P) \to \A(P^{*})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The pieces of $P^{*}$ are exactly the complements of those of $P$, so the biconditional follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:clause} quantified universally. For the set identity: if $b \models P^{*}$, apply the biconditional to $a = \sigma(b)$, using $\sigma\sigma = \mathrm{id}$, to get $\sigma(b) \models P$ and hence $b \in \sigma(\A(P))$; conversely if $b = \sigma(a)$ with $a \models P$ then $b \models P^{*}$. Cardinalities agree because $\sigma$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Solvability invariance]
$\A(P) \ne \emptyset \iff \A(P^{*}) \ne \emptyset$.
\end{corollary}

This classical statement is the image of Theorem~\ref{thm:transport} under ``is the space nonempty?'', i.e.\ its $\pi_{0}$.

\section{The untagged parity theorem}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{combined assembly space} is $\Cc(P) = \A(P) \cup \A(P^{*}) \subseteq \cube$.
\end{definition}

We stress \emph{untagged}. In the disjoint (tagged) union the swap involution is free for trivial reasons and the parity conclusion carries no information about the puzzle's combinatorics. In the untagged union, elements of $\A(P)\cap\A(P^{*})$ are counted once, and one must argue that no collapse creates a fixed point.

\begin{lemma}[Stability]\label{lem:stab}
$\Cc(P)$ is $\sigma$-stable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $a \models P$ then $\sigma(a) \models P^{*}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:transport}. If $a \models P^{*}$, write $a = \sigma(\sigma(a))$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:transport} with $b = \sigma(a)$ to get $\sigma(a) \models P$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Untagged parity]\label{thm:parity}
For every framed puzzle $P$ on $n \ge 1$ variables, $|\Cc(P)|$ is even. The same holds for $|\A(P)\cap\A(P^{*})|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:stab} the set $\Cc(P)$ is $\sigma$-stable, the restriction is an involution, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:free} it is fixed-point free; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:freeparity}. The intersection is $\sigma$-stable by the same two implications taken conjunctively.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Self-dual parity]\label{thm:selfdual}
Let $P$ be a framed puzzle on $n \ge 1$ variables with $\A(P^{*}) = \A(P)$ (in particular if $P^{*} = P$). Then $|\A(P)|$ is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Cc(P) = \A(P)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}.
\end{proof}

Self-duality therefore \emph{strengthens} the conclusion: it is not a case to be excluded but the case in which the free involution acts on a single space rather than exchanging two.

\begin{theorem}[The conjecture as posed]\label{thm:conj}
If $P^{*} \ne P$, then $|\Cc(P)|$ is even.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $n \ge 1$, apply Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}, ignoring the hypothesis. If $n = 0$, every clause piece is the empty list, so $P^{*} = P$, contradicting the hypothesis; the case is vacuous.
\end{proof}

\section{Sharpness: the boundary is \texorpdfstring{$n=0$}{n=0}}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{proposition}
Every framed puzzle on zero variables satisfies $P^{*} = P$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A notch carries a variable index in the empty set, so every clause piece is the empty list and complementation is the identity on it.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
On zero variables the cube is the singleton $\{\varepsilon\}$ and $\sigma(\varepsilon) = \varepsilon$. The empty puzzle satisfies $\A(P) = \{\varepsilon\}$, hence $|\Cc(P)| = 1$, which is odd.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Unique fixed configuration]
The empty assembly on zero variables is the unique fixed point of complementation anywhere in the theory, and it is exactly where parity fails.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}[$n=2$]
Let $P_{1} = (x_{1}) \wedge (x_{2})$. Then $\A(P_{1}) = \{(\T,\T)\}$ and $\A(P_{1}^{*}) = \{(\F,\F)\}$, so $|\Cc(P_{1})| = 2$: one free orbit, and $P_{1}^{*} \ne P_{1}$. Let $P_{2} = (x_{1} \vee \lnot x_{1})$. Then $\A(P_{2}^{*}) = \A(P_{2})$ is the whole cube, of size $4$ --- even, as Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdual} predicts, comprising two free orbits.
\end{example}

\section{Orbit structure, gauge fixing, and sign}

\begin{definition}[Polarity gauge]
For $n \ge 1$ and $S \subseteq \cube$, set $\Gauge(S) = \{\, a \in S : a_{1} = \T \,\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit decomposition]\label{thm:orbit}
Let $n \ge 1$ and let $S \subseteq \cube$ be $\sigma$-stable. Then
\[
  S = \Gauge(S) \sqcup \sigma(\Gauge(S)), \qquad\text{hence}\qquad |S| = 2\,|\Gauge(S)| .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Disjointness:} elements of $\Gauge(S)$ have first coordinate $\T$, those of $\sigma(\Gauge(S))$ have $\F$. \emph{Covering:} let $a \in S$; if $a_{1} = \T$ then $a \in \Gauge(S)$, otherwise $\sigma(a)\in S$ by stability with $\sigma(a)_{1} = \T$, and $a = \sigma(\sigma(a))$. \emph{Reverse inclusion:} $\Gauge(S)\subseteq S$ by definition and $\sigma(\Gauge(S))\subseteq S$ by stability. \emph{Cardinality:} $\sigma$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n \ge 1$, $|\Cc(P)| = 2|\Gauge(\Cc(P))|$, and for a self-dual space $|\A(P)| = 2|\Gauge(\A(P))|$. Parity is recovered with a witnessing section, and $|\Gauge(S)|$ is the number of orbits.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:gaugecube}
$|\Gauge(\cube)| = 2^{n-1}$; no framed puzzle on $n$ variables has more than $2^{n-1}$ complementation orbits of assemblies.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Sign of complementation]\label{thm:sign}
Regard $\sigma$ as a permutation of the $2^{n}$-element cube. For $n \ge 1$,
\[
  \operatorname{sgn}(\sigma) = (-1)^{2^{\,n-1}} .
\]
Hence $\sigma$ is odd exactly when $n = 1$, and even for all $n \ge 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma^{2} = \mathrm{id}$ and $\sigma$ has no fixed points, so $\sigma$ is a product of disjoint transpositions covering the cube; there are $2^{n}/2 = 2^{n-1}$ of them. For $n=1$ the exponent is $1$; for $n \ge 2$ it is even.
\end{proof}

Thus the order-two edge symmetry has nontrivial image in $S_{2^{n}}/A_{2^{n}} \cong \mathbb{Z}/2$ in exactly one dimension.

\section{Complete expressiveness}

\begin{definition}[Exclusion piece]
For $b \in \cube$ let
\[
  E_{b} = \bigl[\,(1,\lnot b_{1}),\,(2,\lnot b_{2}),\,\dots,\,(n,\lnot b_{n})\,\bigr],
\]
the single clause piece exposing, for each variable, the notch milled for the opposite of $b$'s choice.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exclusion]\label{lem:excl}
$E_{b}$ snaps into place under $a$ if and only if $a \ne b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If a notch $(i,\lnot b_{i})$ fits under $a$ then $a_{i} = \lnot b_{i} \ne b_{i}$, so $a \ne b$. Conversely if $a \ne b$ then $a_{i} \ne b_{i}$ for some $i$, i.e.\ $a_{i} = \lnot b_{i}$, so that notch fits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complete expressiveness]\label{thm:express}
For every $S \subseteq \cube$ let $P_{S} = [\,E_{b} : b \notin S\,]$. Then $\A(P_{S}) = S$, and $P_{S}$ has exactly $2^{n} - |S|$ clause pieces. Consequently $P \mapsto \A(P)$ is surjective onto the subsets of the cube.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $a \in S$, every piece $E_{b}$ of $P_{S}$ has $b \notin S$, so $b \ne a$ and $E_{b}$ snaps into place by Lemma~\ref{lem:excl}; hence $a \models P_{S}$. If $a \notin S$ then $E_{a}$ is a piece of $P_{S}$ and does not snap into place under $a$. The piece count is the number of excluded points.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Redundancy and complexity]
$P_{S}$ is maximally redundant: one piece per excluded assembly. Nothing here bounds the minimal number of pieces realising $S$; the results below concern framed puzzles of unbounded description size. For puzzles of bounded description size, counting assemblies is $\#\mathrm{P}$-hard by the standard parsimonious reduction from satisfiability, and no comparable spectrum theorem should be expected.
\end{remark}

\section{Exact spectra}

\begin{theorem}[Single-puzzle spectrum]\label{thm:single}
For every $k \le 2^{n}$ there is a framed puzzle $P$ on $n$ variables with $|\A(P)| = k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $S$ with $|S| = k$ and take $P_{S}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Odd counts occur]
For every $n$ there is a puzzle with an odd number of assemblies (take $k=1$).
\end{corollary}

Hence the parity theorem could not have been about a single assembly space: it is intrinsically about the complement-stable union.

\begin{lemma}[Stable sets of prescribed even size]\label{lem:stableset}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $2k \le 2^{n}$. There exists a $\sigma$-stable $S \subseteq \cube$ with $|S| = 2k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The gauge of the cube has $2^{n-1} \ge k$ elements (Corollary~\ref{cor:gaugecube}); choose $T$ a $k$-element subset and set $S = T \cup \sigma(T)$. Stability is clear, and $T$, $\sigma(T)$ are disjoint by first coordinate, so $|S| = 2k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Combined spectrum]\label{thm:combined}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $2k \le 2^{n}$. Some framed puzzle on $n$ variables has $|\Cc(P)| = 2k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $S$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:stableset} and $P = P_{S}$. Then $\A(P) = S$ and $\A(P^{*}) = \sigma(S) = S$, so $\Cc(P) = S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact characterisation of combined counts]\label{thm:spectrum}
Let $n \ge 1$. A natural number $m$ equals $|\Cc(P)|$ for some framed puzzle on $n$ variables if and only if $m$ is even and $m \le 2^{n}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Necessity: evenness is Theorem~\ref{thm:parity}, the bound holds since $\Cc(P) \subseteq \cube$. Sufficiency: write $m = 2k$ and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:combined}.
\end{proof}

This is the sharp boundary: freeness forces evenness, expressiveness forces nothing else. Complementation contributes exactly one bit of global information about a framed puzzle.

\begin{theorem}[Self-dual spectrum]\label{thm:selfdualspec}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $2k \le 2^{n}$. There is a framed puzzle $P$ with $\A(P^{*}) = \A(P)$ and $|\A(P)| = 2k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The puzzle $P_{S}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:combined} has $\A(P_{S}^{*}) = \sigma(S) = S = \A(P_{S})$.
\end{proof}

\section{The density of self-duality}

\begin{definition}
$\mathcal{S}_{n} = \{\, S \subseteq \cube : \sigma(S) \subseteq S \,\}$, the family of complement-stable subsets.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Gauge parameterisation]\label{lem:param}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $G = \Gauge(\cube)$, so $|G| = 2^{n-1}$. The maps $S \mapsto S \cap G$ and $T \mapsto T \cup \sigma(T)$ are mutually inverse bijections between $\mathcal{S}_{n}$ and the powerset of $G$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Well-definedness:} $S \cap G \subseteq G$, and $T \cup \sigma(T)$ is stable since $\sigma(T\cup\sigma(T)) = \sigma(T)\cup T$. \emph{Reconstruction:} one inclusion is stability of $S$; conversely for $a \in S$ either $a_{1} = \T$ and $a \in S\cap G$, or $\sigma(a) \in S\cap G$ and $a = \sigma(\sigma(a))$. \emph{Section:} for $T \subseteq G$, elements of $T$ lie in $G$ while elements of $\sigma(T)$ have first coordinate $\F$, so $(T\cup\sigma(T))\cap G = T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact count of self-dual assembly spaces]\label{thm:density}
For $n \ge 1$,
\[
  |\mathcal{S}_{n}| = 2^{\,2^{\,n-1}}, \qquad |\mathcal{S}_{n}|^{2} = 2^{\,2^{\,n}} = \#\{\text{all assembly spaces}\},
\]
and in particular $|\mathcal{S}_{n}| < 2^{2^{n}}$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:param}, $|\mathcal{S}_{n}| = 2^{|G|} = 2^{2^{n-1}}$; squaring gives $2^{2^{n-1}+2^{n-1}} = 2^{2^{n}}$. Every subset of the cube is an assembly space (Theorem~\ref{thm:express}) and there are $2^{2^{n}}$ subsets. Strictness holds since $2^{n-1} < 2^{n}$.
\end{proof}

Self-duality is thus exactly a square-root condition: doubly exponentially rare, yet by Theorem~\ref{thm:selfdualspec} realised in every admissible size. Small cases: $|\mathcal{S}_{1}| = 2$ of $4$; $|\mathcal{S}_{2}| = 4$ of $16$; $|\mathcal{S}_{3}| = 16$ of $256$.

\begin{remark}
This counts stable assembly \emph{spaces}, not self-dual puzzles as syntactic objects, of which there are infinitely many. Each stable space has a canonical realising puzzle, namely $P_{S}$.
\end{remark}

\section{Cyclic generalisation: \texorpdfstring{$d$}{d} interlock depths}

The number two entered only through the order of the involution.

\begin{definition}
A $d$-ary literal input is a pair $(i,t) \in \{1,\dots,n\}\times\Zd$; clause pieces and puzzles are lists as before, and a $d$-ary assembly is a map $a : \{1,\dots,n\} \to \Zd$. The input $(i,t)$ fits under $a$ iff $a_{i} = t$. Write $\A_{d}(P) \subseteq (\Zd)^{n}$ for the assembly space.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Depth shift]
For $t \in \Zd$, deepen every mill by $t$: on pieces $(i,s)\mapsto(i,s+t)$, giving $P^{+t}$; on assemblies $\tau_{t}(a)_{i} = a_{i}+t$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact transport, cyclic form]\label{lem:cyctrans}
$\tau_{t}(a) \models P^{+t}$ if and only if $a \models P$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At the input level $a_{i}+t = s+t \iff a_{i} = s$; the shift is a bijection of inputs of $c$ onto inputs of $c^{+t}$, so the equivalence lifts.
\end{proof}

Define the combined space of all depth shifts,
\[
  \Cc_{d}(P) = \bigcup_{t \in \Zd} \A_{d}\bigl(P^{+t}\bigr) \subseteq (\Zd)^{n},
\]
which is shift-stable by Lemma~\ref{lem:cyctrans} together with $(P^{+s})^{+t} = P^{+(s+t)}$. Set $\Gauge_{d}(S) = \{a \in S : a_{1} = 0\}$.

\begin{theorem}[Cyclic orbit decomposition]\label{thm:cycorbit}
Let $n \ge 1$ and $S \subseteq (\Zd)^{n}$ be shift-stable. Then $(g,t) \mapsto \tau_{t}(g)$ is a bijection $\Gauge_{d}(S)\times\Zd \to S$, so $|S| = d\cdot|\Gauge_{d}(S)|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Surjectivity:} given $a \in S$, put $t = a_{1}$ and $g = \tau_{-t}(a)$; then $g \in S$ by stability, $g_{1} = 0$, and $\tau_{t}(g) = a$. \emph{Injectivity:} if $\tau_{t}(g) = \tau_{t'}(g')$ with $g_{1} = g'_{1} = 0$, evaluating at coordinate $1$ gives $t = t'$, and cancelling gives $g = g'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cyclic divisibility]\label{thm:cyclic}
For $n \ge 1$ and any $d$-ary framed puzzle $P$, $|\Cc_{d}(P)| = d\cdot|\Gauge_{d}(\Cc_{d}(P))|$; in particular $d \mid |\Cc_{d}(P)|$.
\end{theorem}

For $d = 2$ this is the tab--blank parity theorem, expressed additively. At $n = 0$ the cube is a single point fixed by every shift and $|\Cc_{d}(P)| = 1$, so $n \ge 1$ remains the exact boundary for every $d$: the obstruction is dimensional, not symmetry-theoretic. The constraint on solution counts is the order of the group acting freely on the configuration space.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Assembly enumeration.} Iterate over all $2^{n}$ assignments, testing each of the $m$ pieces; cost $O(2^{n}L)$ where $L$ is the total notch count.
\item \textbf{Combined space and parity check.} Compute $\A(P)$, $\A(P^{*})$, union them; the count must be even for $n \ge 1$.
\item \textbf{Gauge/orbit extraction.} For stable $S$ output $\Gauge(S)$ and the orbit list $\{(g,\sigma g)\}$; cost $O(|S|)$; the witness form of parity.
\item \textbf{Prescribed-space realisation.} Output $P_{S} = [E_{b} : b\notin S]$; cost $O((2^{n}-|S|)n)$.
\item \textbf{Even-count synthesis.} From $2k \le 2^{n}$ take $k$ gauge points, adjoin complements, realise; gives $|\Cc| = 2k$ exactly.
\item \textbf{Stable-space census.} Enumerate $T \subseteq G$ and output $T\cup\sigma(T)$; $2^{2^{n-1}}$ steps, optimal.
\item \textbf{Cyclic divisibility check.} Enumerate $(\Zd)^{n}$ for each of the $d$ shifts and union; cost $O(d^{\,n+1}L)$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Where the symmetry lives}

The conceptual correction supplied here is that complementation does not act on puzzles; it acts on the configuration space, and on assembly spaces only by transport. Hence freeness is a property of the cube ($n\ge1$), independent of the puzzle; self-duality is not a fixed-point condition but a collapse of two transported spaces onto one, which strengthens the parity conclusion; and the exceptional case is dimensional.

\subsection{Constraint versus expressiveness}

The results split into a constraint half (Theorems~\ref{thm:transport}, \ref{thm:parity}, \ref{thm:selfdual}, \ref{thm:orbit}, \ref{thm:sign}, \ref{thm:cyclic}) and an expressiveness half (Theorems~\ref{thm:express}, \ref{thm:single}, \ref{thm:combined}, \ref{thm:selfdualspec}, \ref{thm:density}). Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} is the unique point where they meet, and they meet exactly.

\subsection{Layers of shadow}

\[
  \text{orbit decomposition} \Rightarrow \text{exact count } 2|\Gauge| \Rightarrow \text{parity} \Rightarrow \text{equinumerosity} \Rightarrow \text{solvability invariance}.
\]

\subsection{Limitations}

Spectrum and census results concern puzzles of unbounded description size; for bounded size, counting assemblies is $\#\mathrm{P}$-hard. Self-duality is used at the level of assembly spaces. The gauge depends on a chosen distinguished variable, though any variable serves and the gauges are canonically bijective. The cyclic model is additive over $\Zd$, so $d=2$ reproduces the Boolean statement through $\{\T,\F\}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2$, a parallel rather than a literal generalisation. Divisibility is by the order of the shift group only.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Minimal piece count.} The realisation $P_{S}$ uses $2^{n}-|S|$ pieces, maximally redundant. We conjecture the minimum number of clause pieces realising $S$ equals the minimum number of clauses in a CNF representing the indicator of $S$, and that for complement-stable $S$ this minimum is attained by a complement-stable multiset of pieces --- complementation acts on the space of realisations, so a minimal realisation of a stable space should be symmetrisable at no cost.

\paragraph{Symmetric complexity.} Is there a stable $S$ whose smallest symmetric realisation is strictly larger than its smallest realisation? A negative answer would say symmetry is free in this constraint language.

\paragraph{Bounded-size spectra.} Fix a piece budget $m$: which combined counts are achievable? Parity persists, expressiveness does not.

\paragraph{Non-abelian mill symmetries.} Which finite groups $G$ act freely on configuration spaces $X^{n}$ by re-milling operations, and does $|G| \mid |\Cc|$ persist? Freeness, not commutativity, is what the argument needs.

\paragraph{Refined invariants beyond parity.} Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum} shows parity is the only cardinality constraint. Is there structure on the combined space itself --- as a graph under Hamming adjacency, say --- that complementation constrains and cardinality does not see?

\paragraph{Weighted versions.} Assign weights to assemblies and ask when complementation forces a weighted count to vanish or be divisible, connecting the present orbit argument to sign-reversing-involution techniques in enumerative combinatorics.

\section{Conclusion}

Global tab--blank complementation is an order-two transport of complete assembly spaces whose freeness is a property of the Boolean cube rather than of any puzzle. From that one fact descend equinumerosity of the two assembly spaces; evenness of their untagged union for $n \ge 1$ without any non-self-duality hypothesis; evenness of the assembly count of any self-dual puzzle; an explicit orbit decomposition with a computable polarity gauge; and the identification of the unique fixed configuration as the empty assembly on zero variables, precisely where parity fails.

Against this, framed puzzles are completely expressive: one exclusion piece forbids exactly one assembly, so every subset of the Boolean cube is an assembly space. The two halves meet exactly. For $n \ge 1$ the achievable combined assembly counts are precisely the even numbers at most $2^{n}$; single counts fill the whole interval; self-dual spaces occur in every even size and number exactly $2^{2^{n-1}}$, the square root of all $2^{2^{n}}$ assembly spaces. Replacing tab and blank by $d$ interlock depths replaces parity by divisibility by $d$.

Complementation therefore contributes to a framed puzzle exactly one bit of global information --- the parity of its combined assembly count --- and, provably, not one bit more.

